Analyzer is going well. Some things are slightly annoying, like the deep-deep-deepness of the parse tree. I often have to do three or four indexings in a row to get the data I need:

	local nnode = node[4]
	if nnode[1][1][1] == 'Name' then
		self.name = nnode[1][1][2]

with magic numbers that come from the organisation of the grammar. But with a few assert() on the rule names, it goes well. Now terminals are tables with a single string in it, repetitions are lists of nodes, and token themselves are another level of table around the actual string. In the future bootstrapped sool compiler, tokens, nodes and terminals will all be objects that magically present the same interface, and the level of deepness should be reduced.

On a totally unrelated (and premature) topic, I thought of a potentially nice anonymous function syntax. For instance in a "strip" method for containers, or the classic "map" and "fold" from functionnal programming. With a list

list = /1,2,3\

instead of a heavy

list.strip(function(num x) return x > 1 end)

it could be as simple as:

list.strip(|x > 1|)

The signature of the function can be inferred from the method it is used in (here a list of numbers would have a strip method that expects a num::num function). The arguments are taken to be the unknown names that appear in the function, in order (here x). And the return value is the value of the last statement-expression.

Of course it implies a whole lot of new things, like having a built-in "function" type, which itself contains a return type and an argument type. To be any useful it would also actually need closures. And because of the prototype inferring, it should be always passed directly as argument to a method (or at least have a delayed type inferring like locals). Plus that breaks the "everything is an object", or else we need to make these functions "first class" objects that can be assigned, passed as argument, and sent messages to.

Fun fun. But that's for later. Aligator.
